  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">For 1 - 3, determine whether each ordered pair is a solution to the inequality \(y >
        x − 1\).</para>
   
        <exercise id="_4f57fb23-4a13-46c7-bade-4751d590d15b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">\((0,1)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00004">Correct! Yes</para>
            <para id="para-00005">Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00006">The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((0, 1)\) into the
              inequality \(y > x − 1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement,
              which makes it a solution. \(1 > 0 − 1\) \(1 > 0 − 1\) \(1 > − 1\) \(1 > −
              1\) 1 is greater than -1. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true. 1 is
              greater than -1. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_2e0361f0-9152-4dfd-91ed-b78c12827035" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">\((−4,−1)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00008">Correct! Yes.</para>
            <para id="para-00009">Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00010">The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((−4,−1)\) into the
              inequality \(y > x − 1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement,
              which makes it a solution. \(-1 > -4 − 1\) \(-1 > -4 − 1\) \(-1 > −5\) \(-1
              > −5\) -1 is greater than -5. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.
              -1 is greater than -5. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="b0de4861-3d99-462f-b776-39e212e0fc77" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00011">\((4,2)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00012">Correct! No.</para>
            <para id="para-00013">Try again. Revist activity 2.11.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00014">The correct answer is No. Substitute the ordered pair \((4, 2)\) into the
              inequality \(y > x − 1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement,
              which makes it a solution. \(2 > 4 − 1\) \(2 > 4 − 1\) \(2 > 3\) \(2 > 3\)
              2 is not greater than 3. No, it is not a solution because the inequality is not true.
              2 is not greater than 3. No, it is not a solution because the inequality is not true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7b40b269-3d20-4270-a430-706c08eb23ab" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00015">\((0,0)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00016">Correct! Yes, \((0,0)\) is a solution to the inequality.</para>
            <para id="para-00017">Try again. Remember to substitute the coordinates into the inequality to check if
              it holds true.</para>
            <para id="para-00018">The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((0, 0)\) into the
              inequality \(y > x - 3\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement.
              \(0 > 0 -3\) \(0 > 0 -3\) \(0 > -3\) \(0 > -3\) 0 is indeed greater than
              -3, making it a solution to the inequality. 0 is indeed greater than -3, making it a
              solution to the inequality.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1fc70666-6c63-4cc0-8df5-53817254d3b5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00019">\((2,1)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00020">Correct! No, \((2,1)\) is not a solution to the inequality.</para>
            <para id="para-00021">Try again. Check the inequality with the given coordinates.</para>
            <para id="para-00022">The correct answer is No. Substitute the ordered pair \((2, 1)\) into the
              inequality \(y > x - 3\) to determine if it's a solution. \(1 > 2 -3\) \(1 >
              2 -3\) \(1 > -1\) \(1 > -1\) Although 1 is greater than -1, the coordinates
              \((2,1)\) do not satisfy the original inequality \(y > x - 3\), therefore it is not
              a solution. Although 1 is greater than -1, the coordinates \((2,1)\) do not satisfy
              the original inequality \(y > x - 3\), therefore it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6a8c18e6-92fc-43ad-ba1e-d1351ac96415" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00023">\((−1,−5)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00024">Correct! No</para>
            <para id="para-00025">Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00026">The correct answer is No. Substitute the ordered pair \((-1,-5)\) into the
              inequality \(y > x − 3\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement,
              which makes it a solution. \(-5 > -1 −3\) \(-5 > -1 −3\) \(-5 > -4\) \(-5
              > -4\) -5 is not greater than -4. No, it is not a solution because the inequality
              is not true. -5 is not greater than -4. No, it is not a solution because the
              inequality is not true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f12e8b48-6d40-47d3-ae44-2f85f62d6c21" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00027">\((1,1)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00028">Correct! Yes</para>
            <para id="para-00029">Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00030">The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((1, 1)\) into the
              inequality \(2x+3y>2\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement,
              which makes it a solution. \(2(1)+3(1) > 2\) \(2(1)+3(1) > 2\) \(5>2\)
              \(5>2\) 5 is greater than 2. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.
              5 is greater than 2. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_2c7ac670-7c24-412c-ade2-c8818efc0611" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00031">\((4,−3)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00032">Correct! No</para>
            <para id="para-00033">Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00034">The correct answer is No. Substitute the ordered pair \((4,-3)\) into the
              inequality \(2x+3y>2\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement,
              which makes it a solution. \(2(4)+3(-3) > 2\) \(2(4)+3(-3) > 2\) \(8-9 > 2\)
              \(8-9 > 2\) \(-1 > 2\) \(-1 > 2\) -1 is not greater than 2. No, it is not a
              solution because the inequality is not true. -1 is not greater than 2. No, it is not a
              solution because the inequality is not true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <!--retries
      set to 2 in this section-->
      <!--BEGIN
      PRACTICE PROBLEM MULTIPLE CHOICE -->

        <exercise id="ded25a4b-2d81-4d4d-9bff-397f05af0ea0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00035">Which inequality represents the graph?</para>
            
              <media alt="graph of y &lt; 3x − 4">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_13_10_01.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
            <para id="para-00036">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00037">\(y\;\leq\;3x\;-\;4\)</para>
            <para id="para-00038">\(y &lt; 3x − 4\)</para>
            <para id="para-00039">\(y > 3x − 4\)</para>
            <para id="para-00040">\(y\;\geq\;3x\;-\;4\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00041">Correct! \(y &lt; 3x − 4\)</para>
            <para id="para-00042">Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00043">The correct answer is \(y &lt; 3x − 4\). The graph has a dotted line, so any
              answers that have \(\geq\) or\(\leq\) can be eliminated. The two remaining options are
              \(y>3x−4\) and \(y>3x−4\). The two remaining options are \(y>3x−4\) and
              \(y>3x−4\). If we set \(x\) to 0, we can see that the \(y\)-coordinate is -4. Look
              at the shading in the graph to determine if y>-4 or \(y>-4\). The graph is
              shaded to the right and down so \(y>-4\). Therefore the equation \(y>3x−4\) is
              the correct answer. If we set \(x\) to 0, we can see that the \(y\)-coordinate is -4.
              Look at the shading in the graph to determine if y>-4 or \(y>-4\). The graph is
              shaded to the right and down so \(y>-4\). Therefore the equation \(y>3x−4\) is
              the correct answer.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <!--END
      PRACTICE PROBLEM MULTIPLE CHOICE -->
      <para id="para-00044">For questions 10 and 11, use this graph of the equation \(2y−x=1\) as a guide to answer
        the questions.</para>
      
        <media alt="Graph of a line.">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_13_10_02.svg"/>
        </media>
      
      <!--Retries
      set to 1 in this section-->

        <exercise id="_7f152e76-fb43-4be0-8bfa-4843d43903b0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00045">Is the point \((0, \frac12)\) a solution to the equation?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00046">Correct! Yes</para>
            <para id="para-00047">Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00048">The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((0, \frac{1}{2})\) into
              the equation \(2y−x=1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement,
              which makes it a solution. \(2(\frac{1}{2})−0=1\)
              \(2(\frac{1}{2})−0=1\)\(1-0=1\)\(1-0=1\)\(1=1\)\(1=1\) 1 equals 1. Yes, it is a
              solution because the equation is true. 1 equals 1. Yes, it is a solution because the
              equation is true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="efb0a1f2-d550-4912-bfe9-35a088fe95d5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00049">Is the point \((0,2)\) a solution to the inequality \(2y − x > 1\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00050">Correct! Yes</para>
            <para id="para-00051">Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00052">The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((0, 2)\) into the
              inequality \(2y−x>1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement,
              which makes it a solution. \(2(2)−0>1\) \(2(2)−0>1\) \(4-0>1\) \(4-0>1\)
              \(4>1\) \(4>1\) 4 is greater than 1. Yes, it is a solution because the
              inequality is true. 4 is greater than 1. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality
              is true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <!--Retries
      set tp 1 this section-->

        <exercise id="_04793c5c-6570-44f1-9a26-ee459ae51b57" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00053">Consider the linear equation \(2y − 3x = 5\). The pair \((-1,1)\) is a solution to
              the equation. Which of the following is also a solution to the equation?</para>
            <para id="para-00054">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00055">\((1, 5)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00056">\((3, 7)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00057">\((5, 11)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00058">\((-3, -1)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00059">Correct! (3, 7)</para>
            <para id="para-00060">Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00061">The correct answer is (3, 7).</para>
            <para id="para-00062">Substitute the ordered pair \((3, 7)\) into the equation \(2y−3x=5\) to determine
              if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution.
              \(2(7)−3(3)=5\) \(2(7)−3(3)=5\) \(14-9=5\) \(14-9=5\) \(5=5\) \(5=5\) 5 equals 5. The
              ordered pair (3, 7) is a solution because the equation is true. 5 equals 5. The
              ordered pair (3, 7) is a solution because the equation is true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6bb26149-83d5-417f-be34-7ca51c59d2bd" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <para id="para-00063">The boundary line on the graph represents the equation \( 5x + 2y = 6\). Write an
              inequality that is represented by the graph.</para>
            
              <media alt="graph with boundary line that represents the equation 5x + 2y = 6">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_13_10_03.svg"/>
              </media>
            
            <para id="para-00064">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00065">\(5x + 2y &lt; -6\)</para>
            <para id="para-00066">\(-5x - 2y &lt; 6\)</para>
            <para id="para-00067">\(5x + 2y &lt; 2\)</para>
            <para id="para-00068">\(5x + 2y &lt; 6\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">
            <para id="para-00069">Correct! \(5x + 2y &lt; 6\)</para>
            <para id="para-00070">Try again. Revist activity 2.11.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00071">The correct answer is \(5x + 2y &lt; 6\). The graph is shaded to the left and down
              and it crosses the \(y\)-axis at 3, so the solution has to show the \(y>3\) when
              \(x=0\). If we plug in 0 into the inequality \(5x+2y>6\) If we plug in 0 into the
              inequality \(5x+2y>6\) \(5(0)+2y>6\) \(5(0)+2y>6\) \(2y>6\) \(2y>6\)
              \(y>3\) \(y>3\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
